  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nAt the start of the long-range forecast, guidance consensus is that \nthe axis of the upper-level ridge responsible for our early-week \nwarmup will have retrograded westward to be centered over the Great \nPlains. This will place the CWA beneath northwesterly flow aloft, \nand hold in place an area of surface high pressure over the \nNortheast. The northwesterly flow aloft will keep low to mid-level \ntemperatures over the CWA steady through at least Thursday-Friday, \nwith the positioning of the surface high having winds weakly south-\nsoutheasterly. Neither factors favor the warming temperatures \nreflected in the NBM through this portion of the period, and an in-\ndepth analysis of this guidance shows that it is being pulled warm \nby the usually warm-biased GFS/GEFS and bias-corrected guidance that \nis being trained on our anomalous warmth of the last couple of \nmonths. Mid-level clouds associated with subtle disturbances \ncresting the ridge Wednesday into Thursday may help to further bring \nafternoon temperatures down from their current forecast. Despite all \nof this, the NBM is still within the range of reasonable outcomes \n(albeit on the far warm side), and targeted changes will be made \nwith decreasing lead time as ensemble spread narrows. \n\nAs we get into the weekend, both deterministic models and ensemble \nclusters show the upper-level ridge amplifying as it's pushed by a \ntrough ejecting into the central CONUS. Despite this consensus, the \nphasing of the trough and ridge among guidance Friday-Sunday varies \ndramatically from model to model, leading to very low confidence in \nour local weather through the weekend. Despite the variety of \nsolutions, nearly all global ensemble members and deterministic \nmodels have the main thrust of the trough and associated shortwaves \nmoving north of the CWA. This supports a lack of notable FROPA and \ntemperatures holding steady through the weekend compared to the \nmiddle and end of the week.",
